CYCLE=0
IDEA=$'Title: M3 follow-up batch — plan-mode write gate + teammate telemetry hardening\n\nThis slice closes four post-M3 follow-ups recorded in doc/BACKLOG.md (all under\nthe [2026-06-17] headings). They batch together because the two substantive\nfixes both touch claude_crew/sdk_teammate.py (the SDK-teammate per-turn loop,\nhooks, and death/telemetry path) — one planned unit so the reviewer sees the\ncross-cutting sdk_teammate.py concerns once. The other two are test-only and\ndisjoint. This is hardening, not new product surface.\n\nIMPORTANT framing — verify before you build. One of these items (stderr\ntelemetry) was logged in the backlog as "never populated / null in practice,"\nbut that was a STALE-SERVER observation during the M3 run, not a code gap. The\ncapture machinery is ALREADY fully implemented and tested. Confirm the current\nstate of each item against the code before deciding what work remains — do not\nre-implement what already exists.\n\n================================================================================\nDELIVERABLE 1 (HIGH — the centerpiece): claude-crew-side plan-mode write gate\n================================================================================\n\nProblem: a teammate spawned with permission_mode="plan" can still WRITE files in\nheadless SDK subprocess sessions. As of claude-agent-sdk 0.1.68 / Claude Code\nCLI 2.1.177, plan mode presents an approval UI instead of silently blocking;\nheadless, that UI is a no-op, so the Write goes through. Any coordinator that\nspawns teammates with permission_mode:plan as a safety gate against mutations is\nNOT actually gated. This is a silent safety regression (severity HIGH).\n\nThe wiring is CORRECT today — claude_crew/sdk_teammate.py sets\nopts_kwargs["permission_mode"] (~line 1448) and --permission-mode plan reaches\nthe CLI. The behavioral contract changed upstream. So the fix is NOT wiring; it\nis a claude-crew-SIDE enforcement layer that does not depend on the SDK\'s plan\ngate.\n\nDesired outcome: when a teammate declares permission_mode:plan (whether via the\nspawn-time arg or the role pack), claude-crew itself denies the mutating tools\n(Write, Edit, NotebookEdit, and any other file/state mutators in the standard\ntoolset) so the write CANNOT happen, regardless of SDK behavior. There is\nalready a PreToolUse hook in sdk_teammate.py (the callback around line 767, the\nhook registration around line 1390) and a disallowed_tools wiring path\n(role-pack disallowedTools around lines 1457-1461) — the planner should evaluate\nboth mechanisms and choose the one that is robust, testable, and least likely to\nsilently break (a PreToolUse deny-hook that returns a permissionDecision of\n"deny" is the more surgical option; disallowed_tools is coarser but simpler).\nConsider that some plan-mode teammates legitimately use non-mutating tools\n(Read, Grep, Bash for read-only inspection) — the gate must block MUTATION, not\nneuter the teammate entirely. Decide and document exactly which tools are\ndenied under plan mode.\n\nValidation is non-negotiable here: this needs a LIVE SDK test that spawns a real\nteammate with permission_mode="plan", asks it to write a file, and asserts the\nfile does NOT exist afterward. There is already an xfail test for exactly this:\ntests/test_live_sdk.py::test_plan_mode_blocks_file_write_and_cwd_works\n(currently @pytest.mark.xfail(strict=False) at ~line 232). Once the claude-crew\ngate exists and works, flip that test to strict (remove xfail / make it a hard\npass). Also add implementation-level coverage (the PreToolUse deny path or the\ndisallowed_tools set) so the gate is proven at both layers.\n\nAlso: update CLAUDE.md "Known limitations" — the current text documents the\nplan-mode regression as an open limitation. Once fixed, that note should reflect\nthe claude-crew-side enforcement (so it no longer reads as "you are not gated").\n\n================================================================================\nDELIVERABLE 2: fix TNM<->subagent correlation (SDK 0.1.68)\n================================================================================\n\nProblem: in SDK 0.1.68 a subagent dispatch produces a TaskNotificationMessage\n(TNM) whose tool_use_id differs from the tool_use_id the PostSubagentUse hook\nsees for the same dispatch. So claude_crew/sdk_teammate.py\'s\n_task_notifs_by_tool_use_id map never matches at _end_turn, which logs\n"_end_turn: no TNM for subagent tool_use_id=... defaulting outcome from hook"\n(around line 1114). The TNM is recorded keyed by tool_use_id in\n_collect_response_text (the msg.tool_use_id gate around line 364).\n\nThis is BENIGN today — the hook-outcome fallback still sets the correct outcome\nand last_subagent_completed, so subagents succeed — but F7\'s precise TNM-based\noutcome telemetry is dead and the warning is noise.\n\nDesired outcome: correlate TNM to its subagent by a stable key that actually\nmatches in 0.1.68 — task_id, or arrival-order per active Task call — instead of\ntool_use_id. Add a regression test asserting the TNM is found (i.e. the\n"no TNM for subagent" fallback warning does NOT fire for a normal subagent\ndispatch). Investigate the real TNM/hook fields available in 0.1.68 before\nchoosing the correlation key; do not guess.\n\nCleanup tied to this: two live tests were narrowed during the M3 greening to\ntolerate the benign warning — they currently assert only on real\n"subagent failure: status=" warnings rather than the full warning filter:\ntests/ test_pack_end_to_end and test_user_and_project_agents_invokable (search\nthe live test files for the narrowed filter). Once correlation is fixed and the\n"no TNM" warning no longer fires, RESTORE the full warning filter in those two\ntests so they once again assert a clean warning-free run.\n\n================================================================================\nDELIVERABLE 3 (test-only + backlog): stderr telemetry — correct stale backlog,\nadd a forced-crash strengthening test\n================================================================================\n\nVERIFY FIRST: the stderr-capture feature is ALREADY shipped (it landed as\n"teammate-death-diagnostics" on 2026-06-09). The full path exists and is tested:\n- claude_crew/sdk_teammate.py: _on_stderr_line callback registered as the SDK\n  stderr sink (opts_kwargs["stderr"] = self._on_stderr_line, ~line 1515), a\n  bounded _stderr_ring buffer, _stderr_tail_redacted(), and\n  snap["stderr_tail"] = self._stderr_tail_redacted() (~line 1230).\n- claude_crew/broker.py: the death record reads snap["stderr_tail"] into\n  stderr_tail_at_death (~line 565) and surfaces it on get_teammate_status\n  (~line 1313) and BrokerSnapshot.\n- Tests: test_broker.py AT-5/AT-6 (broker propagation), test_sdk_teammate.py\n  AT2/AT3 (redaction + empty), AT7 (~line 3716: a ProcessError death emits the\n  WARNING with exc_class/exit_code/last_tool/stderr_tail), and a live test\n  tests/test_live_stderr.py.\n\nSo the backlog entry under [2026-06-17] "Teammate death telemetry: capture &\nsurface subprocess stderr" claiming stderr_tail_at_death is "declared but never\npopulated; null in practice" is STALE — it was observing the M3 run\'s stale\nrunning server (which predated the 2026-06-09 ship; see the existing backlog\nnote at the [2026-06-04] SDK-death entry "the running claude-crew MCP server\npredates the 2026-06-09 teammate-death-diagnostics ship"). The running server\nhas since been restarted onto a current build.\n\nTwo outcomes wanted:\n\n(a) Correct the backlog honestly. Update the [2026-06-17] stderr-telemetry\nentry to record that the capture path is implemented and tested, that the M3\nnull observation was a stale-server artifact, and resolve/close it (mark it\nRESOLVED with the explanation, in the same style as other resolved BACKLOG\nentries — do NOT silently delete it; the audit trail matters).\n\n(b) Add the one genuinely-missing test. The existing live test injects the\nprobe line manually (tm._on_stderr_line(PROBE)) and the AT7 test mocks\nProcessError — neither forces a REAL subprocess non-zero exit and asserts the\ncaptured stderr survives onto the death record. That real-crash path is exactly\nthe M3 scenario that was never live-proven. Add a test (live if it must spawn a\nreal subprocess that crashes; otherwise an integration test that drives the\nreal death path) that forces a teammate subprocess to exit non-zero AND asserts\nstderr_tail_at_death (on the tombstone / get_teammate_status) is non-null and\ncontains the captured subprocess stderr. Note the live-test design caveat\ndocumented in test_live_stderr.py: the claude CLI emits nothing to stderr on a\nnormal turn (all output is stdout JSON) — so the test must induce a real\nstderr-producing crash, not rely on normal-turn stderr.\n\n================================================================================\nDELIVERABLE 4 (test-infra only, disjoint): fix the shutdown-signals flake\n================================================================================\n\nProblem: tests/test_shutdown_signals.py::test_sigterm_triggers_clean_exit_and_\nderegister and ::test_sigint_triggers_clean_exit_and_deregister intermittently\nfail with "_wait_for_registry_entry" timing out at 15s — a spawned\n"python -m claude_crew.cli" subprocess not registering within the window under\nfull-suite load. Passes in isolation and on rerun. Low severity, but it makes\nthe full "uv run pytest" exit non-zero on unlucky runs, which trips the\nvalidation gate and the live-suite-before-merge policy.\n\nDesired outcome: make these two tests resilient to host load — raise /\nparameterize the registration timeout, poll longer with backoff, or serialize\nthese tests so they don\'t race the rest of the suite for CPU. The fix must keep\nthe tests meaningful (they still prove clean SIGTERM/SIGINT deregistration);\njust remove the load-sensitivity. Validate by running them repeatedly under\nconcurrent load and confirming they no longer flake.\n\n================================================================================\nCONSTRAINTS / NON-NEGOTIABLES (whole slice)\n================================================================================\n\n- Honest tests only. No gamed-green: fix the real cause or, if something cannot\n  be fixed in scope, an explicit xfail/skip WITH a backlog note — never weaken\n  an assertion to pass.\n- Two-layer validation per the project standard: implementation-level AND one\n  layer above, happy + sad paths, for every behavioral change (Deliverables 1\n  and 2). Deliverables 3 and 4 are test/doc only.\n- Deliverable 1 ships WITH a live SDK test that actually exercises the gate\n  (real teammate, real Write attempt, assert no file) — a green stub suite is\n  not a merge signal for a permission gate. Per this project\'s CLAUDE.md\n  "Run live tests before merging," the live suite covering the touched surface\n  runs before merge.\n- Full-suite validation. Deliverables 1 and 2 change behavior other suites\n  assert on (a removed/renamed WARN, hook wiring, tool-permission defaults), so\n  the validation gate must run the FULL "uv run pytest" (stub) — not a\n  keyword-filtered subset — plus the relevant live tests\n  (CLAUDE_CREW_LIVE_TESTS=1) for Deliverables 1 and 2.\n- Batch-by-shared-surface note for the breakout: Deliverables 1 and 2 both edit\n  claude_crew/sdk_teammate.py, so they must NOT run as parallel same-file tasks\n  — serialize them with a dependency edge (or fold into one task) so they don\'t\n  collide. Deliverables 3 (tests/ + doc/BACKLOG.md) and 4\n  (tests/test_shutdown_signals.py) are disjoint and can run in parallel with\n  each other and with the sdk_teammate.py work.\n\n================================================================================\nOUT OF SCOPE (do not pull in)\n================================================================================\n\n- The two M3 "follow-up" backlog items at [2026-06-17] — "widen the\n  adaptation_diff gate channel from str to structured AdaptationDiff" and\n  "persist adaptation chains to broker/transcript" — are explicitly deferred\n  enhancements, NOT part of this batch. Leave them in the backlog.\n- Do NOT re-implement the stderr-capture feature (Deliverable 3) — it exists.\n  The only code work there is the new forced-crash test; the rest is a backlog\n  correction.\n- Do not redesign the SDK-teammate turn loop, the broker death machinery, or\n  the redaction allowlist beyond what each deliverable strictly needs.\n\n================================================================================\nPROCESS / AUTHORING DIRECTIVES FOR THIS RUN (planner: honor these)\n================================================================================\n\nThis run doubles as a workflow-tuning experiment. Three directives shape how you\nauthor the spec + breakout:\n\n1. SPEC-TO-AT COVERAGE SWEEP (mandatory). For every optional parameter, default\n   flag, and conditional / branching code path your spec introduces or touches,\n   author AT LEAST ONE acceptance test that exercises that specific branch —\n   INCLUDING the negative / "the gate fires" / "the optional path is taken"\n   case, not just the happy path. Concretely for Deliverable 1: the plan-mode\n   gate has multiple branches — plan-mode + mutating tool (must DENY), plan-mode\n   + read-only tool (must ALLOW), non-plan-mode + mutating tool (must ALLOW).\n   Each branch owes ≥1 AT. This sweep exists because the M3 run shipped a HIGH\n   defect — an optional code branch fully specified but with NO acceptance test\n   exercising it; plan-review caught it late. Make the inverse-coverage explicit\n   in the spec so plan-review can verify it at a glance. Add a short\n   "## Branch Coverage" note (use #### sub-bullets, not ## ) mapping each\n   optional/conditional branch to its covering AT id.\n\n2. BREAKOUT STRUCTURE FOR PARALLEL DISPATCH. This run will dispatch tasks in\n   parallel (RR_PARALLELISM>1). Author the Task Breakout so that:\n   - Deliverable 1 (plan-mode gate) and Deliverable 2 (TNM correlation) BOTH\n     edit claude_crew/sdk_teammate.py → put a dependsOn edge between them (or\n     fold them into one task) so they never run as concurrent same-file tasks.\n     Their taskTouches WILL overlap on sdk_teammate.py; that overlap must be\n     serialized by a dependency edge, not left to collide at merge-back.\n   - Deliverable 3 (stderr: tests/ + doc/BACKLOG.md) and Deliverable 4\n     (tests/test_shutdown_signals.py) touch disjoint files from each other and\n     from the sdk_teammate.py pair → no dependency edges; they dispatch in\n     parallel. Give each accurate, non-overlapping taskTouches globs.\n\n3. IMPLEMENTATION-TIER HINTS. The coordinator will right-size the agent to the\n   work via the tier escape-hatch. Set each task\'s implementation tier honestly:\n   - Deliverable 4 (shutdown-flake): a timeout bump / poll-backoff in one test\n     file — likely TRIVIAL or MECHANICAL tier (coordinator may apply directly or\n     via a Haiku implementor; feature-review backstops).\n   - Deliverable 3 (stderr): the backlog correction is trivial prose; the\n     forced-crash test is real reasoning work (subprocess-death path) — if both\n     live in one task, tier it REASONING; if you split the backlog-doc edit from\n     the test, the doc edit is TRIVIAL.\n   - Deliverables 1 and 2: REASONING tier (judgment-heavy: permission semantics,\n     SDK correlation). Full implementor + slice-reviewer.\n   Annotate the tier per task in the breakout so the coordinator\'s tiering is\n   visible and auditable, not guessed.\n\nPointers the planner should read for context: doc/BACKLOG.md (the four\n[2026-06-17] entries + the [2026-06-04] SDK-death entry for the stale-server\nnote), claude_crew/sdk_teammate.py (hook + telemetry + death paths),\nclaude_crew/broker.py (death record), tests/test_live_sdk.py (the plan-mode\nxfail), tests/test_live_stderr.py (the existing stderr live test + its design\ncaveat), tests/test_shutdown_signals.py, and CLAUDE.md (the live-tests-before-\nmerge policy + Known limitations).'
